This $9 trillion number is nonsense.  The more accurate # is ~$2t.

What Citadel is doing is looking at every 9/18 options contract and valuing it as if it was 100 shares of stock. 
Even if the contract is way out of the money and worth 0.

If you were to value contracts using delta i.e. a "stock equivalent value" then the worthless options are marked at zero, deep in-the-money contracts are worth 100 shares of stock, and at-the-money contracts are closer to 50 shares.

As a side note this "equal to 100 shares" is the same thing the SEC does with 13Fs which often grossly overstates the value of fund options holdings.

